g)\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at ![](images/41/41378cd8f4c141c0db7051b606fc860a0c333837e78305a593ce2f5fbd303a32.svg) we obtain\n![](images/84/84190825d65c6d799de0f606c5d0614692d9ec22ba1dc6916a2e6ce6989760d9.svg)\nComputing the determinant ![](images/72/7265b7327f1bfe1fbfb02e958ff01a35790adbc5e1f0984618c97e85e812fca6.svg) we get\n![](images/f0/f0f000997b9e965a8280f51619abfc3c9f6574fbaa6292f045c7d03720693bd9.svg)\nTherefore, ![](images/52/52c679e6f9a0ed40ecf5652d769a673a1cf842a1816d913ddc4e6a3356dc43af.svg) is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, ![](images/a2/a2dcac06a4cf671df0fa43cef1727449fb2ebc57fb824d19cc07da5e75d12176.svg) has a local minimum at ![](images/dc/dcc19bac01833ff23365244490e52b2722741da56e804e9a95b5b264f23216bf.svg) and a saddle point at ![](images/89/897570ce172a9f169127c297c355bc7a446b1df6ee78afe238f5b53dde75a85c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Critical Points of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124653 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124654 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124655 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1045 "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186051048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124735 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ec39ccfa-c0bc-5c06-b1ec-f08770e57898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125006 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5268fbe7-eec3-599a-9bb3-09780fd2d7a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125006 #edb/ref 1074 "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-6x+3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-6y & -6x+3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-6y=0 \\\\ -6x+3{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6y=0\\,⟹\\,x=3y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(3y)+3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -18y+3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3y(-6+y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=3y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=3(0)=0 \\\\ y=6 & \\,⟹\\,x=3(6)=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(18,6).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(18,6).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125067 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125067 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125067 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125067 #edb/ref 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x} & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,0)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125067 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a07adee-cd5f-5906-86f1-fee57d6ba6a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125067 #edb/ref 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=2\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)=({f}^{″}(1,0))_{11}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,0)>0,$ the point $(1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125649 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125649 #edb/ref 1061 45414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125649 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59e0e71c-41de-59e8-af18-15eaf180ea00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125649 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125650 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125650 #edb/ref 1061 45415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125650 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1edc476-8e7a-5e8a-83d5-126f47cd2bfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125650 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125651 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125651 #edb/ref 1061 45418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125651 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5093fcf-6163-5240-9022-125813bd45bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125651 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125652 #edb/ref 1061 45419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1821258-7802-571e-a84f-0738986e608c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125653 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125653 #edb/ref 1061 45420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f254984-e6a9-5246-b99d-34252f2d1d87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125654 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125654 #edb/ref 1061 45423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125654 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c6b333c-1f09-554a-8089-06e97c78f009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125654 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125661 #edb/ref 1061 45457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125661 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "408587e1-74bc-5208-80a7-5977f2bc1961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125661 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125889 #edb/ref 1064 "The **critical points** of a function $f(\\mathbf x)$ are the points $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist. A critical point such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ is called a **stationary point**.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x,y) = x^2 - 2x + y^2+6y.$ The domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2x-2,\n\\qquad\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y+6.\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](images/9e/9ea3f2d1dd0994be64602aeaffcf5d8424f52daa03844e21e65469f779d9b009.svg)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that ![](images/23/23faa4c01362cad11c2ab6ad19045ca44a09460da1c719c8212480f27f21c5b1.svg) or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist:\n\n- Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $x=1$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, $(1,-3)$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\n- $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so there are no more critical points to be found.\n\nTherefore, the only critical point of $f$ is $(1,-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125889 #edb/ref 1065 7876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125889 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "66c2c3d5-6a0a-561b-81c6-ba81419caa61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125889 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125890 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that, for functions of two variables $f(x,y)$, there are typically three possible types of critical points that can occur:\n\n- Local minimum points. For example, the elliptic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ has a minimum point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\n- Local maximum points. For example, the elliptic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = -x^2-y^2$ has a maximum point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/00/008803f43cb2161624f8ef295c70a125b8be64d93184928bd2176a6debaa9f85.png)\n\n- Saddle points. For example, the hyperbolic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2$ has a saddle point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/12/1214b413073ad87a302fb1297cef47208a922e368d680a9f69ec8fb18d9ca8a0.png)\n\nLocal maximum and local minimum points are analogous to what you would have seen in single-variable calculus. \n\nA saddle point occurs when the slopes are zero in all directions, yet it is not a local maximum or minimum. The hyperbolic paraboloid shown above reaches a local maximum at $(0,0)$ along the $xz$-plane, yet this is a local minimum along the $yz$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125890 #edb/ref 1065 7877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125890 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe3c5f00-f720-5573-a01f-c37f0a3e447f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125890 #edb/ref 1067 "Classifying Stationary Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125891 #edb/ref 1064 "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y=0 \\\\ 2y-x=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125891 #edb/ref 1065 7878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125891 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8248e727-7421-5535-ba58-b5ed98f7ac38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125891 #edb/ref 1067 "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125895 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of a function $f(\\mathbf x).$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that if $H(\\mathbf{a}) = \\det(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})) \\neq 0$, then we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe second derivatives test follows from these facts. To see why, we first recall Sylvester's criterion:\n\nThe quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive definite if and only if $\\Delta_1 > 0$ and $\\Delta_2 > 0,$ and\n\n- negative definite if and only if $\\Delta_1 < 0$ and $\\Delta_2 > 0,$\n\nwhere $\\Delta_1$ and $\\Delta_2$ are the first and second principle minors of $A,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice the following:\n\n- The first principal minor of $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta_1 = (\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}))_{11} = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}.\n$$\n\n- The second principal minor of $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)$ is the Hessian determinant\n\n$$\n\\Delta_2 = \\det(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})) = H(\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can go through each case:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) < 0,$ then $\\Delta_2 < 0.$ Hence, by Sylvester's criterion, the Hessian matrix $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive definite nor negative definite, and we conclude that $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) > 0,$ then $\\Delta_2 > 0.$ Then:\n\n- If $\\Delta_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} < 0,$ then $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite by Sylvester's criterion, and we conclude that $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $\\Delta_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} > 0,$ then $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite by Sylvester's criterion, and we conclude that $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125895 #edb/ref 1065 7886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125895 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0bcd9133-9025-5637-8956-90a3b84934a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125895 #edb/ref 1067 "Relating the Second Partial Derivatives Test to the Taylor Polynomial Classification" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428474 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428474 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1944->1899. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428474 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050992 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428475 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/