186045417] [17592186434978 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434978 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a3de3699-ca1f-5183-a0b0-3f92f5e0689d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434979 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce8b2aa5-8481-5045-bb5b-59aa8a2d0aa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186434976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186434956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434980 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf6f62c2-36fa-58b4-83fb-e251b870cb3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434981 #edb/ref 1071 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05076731-f2b5-5426-b032-1f0962af8ee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434981 #edb/ref 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-4x-4y-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-4-2x & x-4-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y-4-2x=0 \\\\ x-4-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny-4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x+4\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4-2(2x+4) & =0 \\\\ -3x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=2x+4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-4\\,⟹\\,y=2(-4)+4=-4.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-4,-4)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-4-2y)=1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4-2)=1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-4-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-4,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4,-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-4,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-4,-4)=det({f}^{″}(-4,-4))=(-2)^{2}-{1}^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-4,-4)=({f}^{″}(-4,-4))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-4,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434982 #edb/ref 1071 "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a336a18-c506-59a2-baef-25499733a8d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434982 #edb/ref 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(xy-3)\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}xy-3=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nxy-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9{(\\frac{3}{x})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}-81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{3}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{-3}=-1,\\,\\,x=3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-3,-1)$ and $(3,1)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-6)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-6)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=-18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -18y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(3,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(3,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,1)=det({f}^{″}(3,1))=2\\cdot (-18)-{6}^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(3,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-3,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-3,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-3,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-3,-1)=det({f}^{″}(-3,-1))=(-2)\\cdot 18-(-6)^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(-3,-1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434983 #edb/ref 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(-1,0)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(-1,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b007310c-a11f-531c-b695-1febe8ef0462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434983 #edb/ref 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,0)=det({f}^{″}(-1,0))=8\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=16>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)=({f}^{″}(-1,0))_{11}=8>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(-1,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,0)>0,$ the point $(-1,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434984 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e309db36-c1df-54f7-9f6d-2cd23acc2d7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186434984 #edb/ref 1074 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣y>0\\},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x+\\frac{2}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y & x+\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{2}{-2x} & =0 \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(-1)=2 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=-2(1)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2).$ We reject the point $(1,-2)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
